o.com); original source CoreWeave 2026-09-30; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] CoreWeave, a cloud company, said on 30 September 2026 that one of its customers runs production workloads on NVIDIA's Vera Rubin system.\\n    Quote: CoreWeave, a cloud company, said on September 30 that one of its customers runs production workloads on the system.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e659; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e864 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management says the company cannot build enough to meet demand, called its fiscal 2028 forecast supply-constrained, expects supply to stay a bottleneck through at least the end of that year, and said on the fiscal Q2 2027 call that its entire supply chain is running flat out.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA cannot build enough to meet demand, management says. Management called its fiscal 2028 forecast supply-constrained, and expects supply to stay a bottleneck through at least the end of that year. Asked on the fiscal Q2 2027 call what holds supply back, management said its entire supply chain is running flat out.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e236; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e894 [fact; wccftech.com, quoting NVIDIA VP Ian Buck; original source NVIDIA and CoreWeave ; from gnews:4875d3af5072a2d9c8532a7f 2026-10-02] NVIDIA and CoreWeave announced at CoreWeave Fully Connected 2026 that the NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 platform is available on CoreWeave Cloud, with NVIDIA VP Ian Buck stating that CoreWeave is bringing Vera Rubin NVL72 into production even as its older V100 GPUs still run customer workloads nearly a decade after Volta launched.\\n    Quote: At CoreWeave Fully Connected 2026, NVIDIA and CoreWeave announce the availability of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 on CoreWeave Cloud. … “NVIDIA accelerated computing delivers value across generations,” said Ian Buck, vice president of hyperscale and high-performance computing at NVIDIA. “CoreWeave’s NVIDIA V100 GPUs are still running customer workloads nearly a decade after Volta launched, even as Core\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e659; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e895 [fact; wccftech.com; original source CoreWeave / Cognition ; from gnews:4875d3af5072a2d9c8532a7f 2026-10-02] Cognition is the first customer to secure installations of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 servers at CoreWeave, and CoreWeave stood up a production Vera Rubin cluster for it in days, with Cognition scaling to thousands of GPUs within nine months; Cognition had earlier benchmarked Vera Rubin against Grace Blackwell in September, recording a 4.8x gain in total token throughput for SWE-2 inference workloads.\\n    Quote: Cognition is the first to secure a hold on these latest server installations at CoreWeave, and has been able to scale to thousands of GPUs within nine months. Earlier in September, Cognition was the first to benchmark Vera Rubin against Grace Blackwell in inference, and was able to record a 4.8x gain in total token throughput for SWE-2 inference workloads. … In days, CoreWeave stood up a productio\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e659; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e907 [party_statement; Daniel Foelber (The Motley Fool); original source Nvidia (guidance); margin/demand attribution by the article's author ; from gnews:d9f1147865cff881040d3dd0 2026-10-02] The article states that Nvidia is already guiding for a 70% increase in revenue for fiscal 2028, and the author attributes continued high margins to what he calls insatiable demand for the Vera Rubin compute platform.\\n    Quote: For fiscal 2028, Nvidia is already guiding for a 70% increase in revenue. … And its margins should remain sky high thanks to insatiable demand for the Vera Rubin compute platform .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e104; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e951 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Supermicro 2026-09-23; from gnews:d38374c4abf4bd13d2cd2ccd 2026-10-02] On Sept. 23, Supermicro said it had started shipping racks built on Nvidia's newest platform, Vera Rubin; each rack carries 72 Rubin GPUs.\\n    Quote: On Sept. 23, Supermicro said it had started shipping racks, the standard cabinets that hold servers inside a data center, built on Nvidia's newest platform, Vera Rubin. Each rack carries 72 Rubin GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e181; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e978 [fact; barchart.com; original source Akamai Technologies 2026-09-24; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] Akamai Technologies said on 24 September 2026 that it had significantly expanded its partnership with Anthropic; Anthropic committed to spend $11.6 billion over seven years on dedicated cloud capacity and related services for its growing CPU workloads, with an option to commit an additional $9 billion if conditions are met, bringing the total value to about $20 billion. The article says the deal does not directly involve Nvidia based on public disclosure.\\n    Quote: A major new agreement between cloud infrastructure provider Akamai Technologies (AKAM) and frontier AI lab Anthropic could add another piece to that story. The nearly $12 billion deal does not directly involve Nvidia, at least based on what has been publicly disclosed so far. … On Sept. 24, cloud infrastructure company Akamai Technologies significantly expanded its partnership with frontier AI lab\\n- e979 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source GF Securities (Henry Huang) ; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] GF Securities analyst Henry Huang said the Akamai-Anthropic CPU cloud deal is expected to be on Nvidia's Vera CPUs, which he said Akamai had previously disclosed as its planned platform for agentic workloads; the article presents this as an expectation, not confirmation.\\n    Quote: \\\"The deal is expected to be on Nvidia's Vera CPUs, which Akamai previously disclosed as its planned platform for agentic workloads,\\\" according to GF Securities analyst Henry Huang.\\n- e980 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] Nvidia said in an August 2026 blog post that it had delivered Vera CPU systems to Anthropic; the article also notes Nvidia had named Anthropic among AI labs planning to adopt Vera at GTC Taipei in May. The delivery is reported as completed, but no volume or revenue is disclosed.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said at GTC Taipei that Anthropic was among the AI labs planning to adopt Vera, and the chipmaker added in an August blog post that it had delivered Vera CPU systems to Anthropic.\\n- e981 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source Akamai Technologies ; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] Akamai expects roughly $5.5 billion of capital expenditures tied to Anthropic's commitment, including an additional $1.7 billion in 2026 to secure and pre-purchase critical supply-chain components; the article says not all of that spending will go toward Nvidia's CPUs because Akamai also needs memory, ABF substrates and other components.\\n    Quote: Akamai expects roughly $5.5 billion in capital expenditures tied to Anthropic's commitment, including an additional $1.7 billion in 2026 to secure and pre-purchase critical supply-chain components. It is worth noting that not all of that spending will go toward Nvidia's CPUs, as Akamai will also need to purchase memory, ABF substrates, and other components.\\n- e982 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) ; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] On Nvidia's Q2 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said trailing-12-month revenue from Grace CPUs had already exceeded $5 billion and that next-generation Vera was in full production; she said Nvidia expects Vera to be deployed by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM, continues to see demand for roughly $20 billion of server CPUs, and preliminarily expects CPU revenue to more than double year-over-year in fiscal 2028.\\n    Quote: Nvidia CFO Colette Kress said on the Q2 earnings call last month that trailing-12-month revenue from Grace CPUs had already exceeded $5 billion, while next-generation Vera was in full production. Kress said Nvidia expects Vera to be deployed by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM, adding that the company continues to see demand for roughly $20 billion of server CPUs. She also\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e632; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1046 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA says Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin and that Vera Rubin delivers 35 times lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra; NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion of revenue per gigawatt for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion per gigawatt for Vera Rubin.\\n    Quote: Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin. NVIDIA says Vera Rubin delivers 35X lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra. NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion per gigawatt of revenue for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e117; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1060 [fact; Brett Schafer, Fool.com; original source Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSMC) ; from gnews:654a1658fd6ce4b1694951fc 2026-10-04] Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSMC), the sole at-scale manufacturer of Nvidia's chips, reported revenue of $143 billion over the past 12 months and revenue up 34% year over year in the most recent quarter, with its high-performance-compute (HPC) segment rising 20% quarter over quarter and now at 66% of the overall business, per Fool.com's account.\\n    Quote: Revenue was $143 billion during the past 12 months and rose 34% year-over-year last quarter. The company is seeing booming demand from its high performance compute (HPC) segment, which increased 20% quarter-over-quarter and now makes up 66% of the overall business.\\n- e1098 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Foxconn (Hon Hai Precision Industry) 2026-10-05; from gnews:3b878790fc298c0b29ba8205 2026-10-05] Foxconn, described as Nvidia's biggest server maker, reported third-quarter 2026 revenue up 47% to T$3.03 trillion ($95.4 billion), above the LSEG SmartEstimate of T$2.83 trillion — a supplier volume signal for Nvidia's AI server business, though the item does not specify which Nvidia platform drove it.\\n    Quote: This morning, Foxconn, Nvidia's biggest server maker, reported third-quarter revenue up 47% to T$3.03 trillion ($95.4 billion), above the LSEG SmartEstimate of T$2.83 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1180; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1101 [fact; fool.com; original source NVIDIA (product status reported by fool.com) ; from gnews:a6cfdb974af32e5f975e0d4f 2026-10-05] NVIDIA's GPU release cadence continues: after Blackwell and Blackwell Ultra launched in recent years, the release of the current Vera Rubin system is now unfolding, according to fool.com's 5 October 2026 article.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has pledged to update its GPUs on an annual basis, and it's following through on this, with Blackwell and Blackwell Ultra launched in recent years and the current release of the Vera Rubin system now unfolding.\\n- e1125 [party_statement; NVIDIA management (via finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:17d71c8bf611131c4216e2eb 2026-10-05] NVIDIA management said each new product generation brings in more money for the same data-center capacity, putting revenue per gigawatt at roughly $18 billion for Hopper, $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\\n    Quote: Each new product generation also brings in more money for the same amount of data center capacity. Management put revenue per gigawatt at roughly $18 billion for Hopper, $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e488; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1148 [party_statement; MarketBeat; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:db898bbdf8476a62d9167361 2026-10-05] NVIDIA itself expects its volume sales to double, an expectation the MarketBeat article ties to the Vera Rubin release and to the next wave of data center construction.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA itself expects volume sales to double, underpinned by the Vera Rubin release.\\n- e1177 [fact; Business Wire (AM Intelligence press release, via Yahoo Finance UK); original source AM Intelligence (AMI) 2026-10-05; from gnews:8ec8d22f933c8556d24c6c9d 2026-10-05] AM Intelligence (AMI), the AI infrastructure platform set up by promoters of Greenko, announced on 5 October 2026 two further firm and binding orders for 20,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs to be deployed as NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 rack-scale systems, on top of its inaugural commitment announced 25 August 2026 for 9,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs at its first AI Factory in Hyderabad. The follow-on capacity totals about 70 MW at Malaysia and India with delivery scheduled for Q2 2027, and the three orders together bring AMI's committed frontier compute to approximately 29,000 Rubin GPUs and close to 100 MW, which AMI describes as making it among the earliest and largest committed buyers of Rubin GPUs outside North America.\\n    Quote: Two further binding orders of ~20,000 NVIDIA GPUs take AMI's committed NVIDIA Vera Rubin capacity to approximately ~29,000 GPUs and close to 100 MW, part of its global development pipeline of ~400 MW … today announced two further firm and binding orders for 20,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs , to be deployed as NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 rack-scale systems. … These orders are in addition to the AMI's inaugural\\n- e1178 [party_statement; Business Wire (AM Intelligence press release, via Yahoo Finance UK); original source AM Intelligence (AMI) 2026-10-05; from gnews:8ec8d22f933c8556d24c6c9d 2026-10-05] AM Intelligence said it plans to bring an additional 300 MW of compute capacity to market in the next 15 months, requiring capital expenditure of more than $20 billion on top of the $6 billion already committed for its initial 100 MW. It said it has developed a pipeline of about 400 MW of Compute-as-a-Service capacity across India, the United States, Europe and Malaysia, that the three Rubin GPU orders are the first committed tranche of that pipeline, and that the balance is under development for delivery in subsequent phases in 2027 and early 2028.\\n    Quote: AMI plans to bring additional 300 MWs of capacity to market in the next 15 months, involving capital expenditure of more than $20+ billion in addition to the $6 billion already committed for the initial $100 MW. … AMI has developed a pipeline of ~400 MW of Compute-as-a-Service capacity across global locations, including India, the United States, Europe and Malaysia. The three orders placed to date\\n\\n[Themes in force, 6]\\n- v10 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?: now rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: its 10–<15 portion is rung 2, its 15–<20 portion is rung 3, and 20 GW reaches rung 4; the midpoint, 15 GW, is rung 3. I forecast below that midpoint because the quantified trades shortfall, the stated net power shortfall, and the two-to-three-\\n- v11 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-03-31) What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?: now rung 5 30 percent or more; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that\\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; that sits at rung 3, Still open, rather than establishing that approval is formally ruled out by the due date. The evidence implies no verified U.S. authorization for Blackwell\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?: now rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. Amazon's approximately $220 billion 2026 capex projection, versus its previous $200 billion level, supports about a $20 billion increase to that reference: roughly $820 billion, still w\\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-30; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or formal decision is supplied, and the reported status is still deliberation rather than approval or rejection. The forecast nevertheless favors rung 1: the specific report says officials indicated purchases were likely to be approved and MIIT intended t\\n- v9 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2027, as shown by their capex disclosures and reported 2027 results?: now rung 3 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's projection of $1.3 trillion for calendar 2027, which sits in rung 3. Barclays' approximately $1.1 trillion figure is for cloud capital spending and is not a like-for-like estimate of the five hyperscalers' data-center capex; the demand evidence, including the new customer-g\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management's expectation that Vera Rubin will make up about 20% of Data Center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027, which is rung 1 because Q3 falls within FY2027. That guidance alone is a forecast, not proof of recognized revenue. But the evidence also says production shipments began in August, Supermicro is shipping Vera Rubin racks, and CoreWeave has the platform running production workloads for Cognition; these make the guided Q3 revenue share more likely to be realized and disclosed by the due date. The supply-bottleneck and possible-delay evidence is a counterweight, but does not outweigh the near-term guidance and customer deployment evidence. The updated capacity and DRAM themes add further caution; they do not move the forecast below rung 1.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n   \"answer\": {\n    \"rung\": 1,\n    \"implied_outcome\": \"The most likely outcome is that NVIDIA recognizes Vera Rubin revenue in Q3 FY2027 and discloses it in its quarterly results, with the guided contribution of about 20% of Data Center revenue. This is inferred from the guide together with production shipments beginning in August and customer production deployments; it is not yet established by a reported result in the evidence shown.\",\n    \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA management's expectation that Vera Rubin will account for about 20% of Data Center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027; because Q3 results are due before 2027-01-31, that reference sits at rung 1. The forecast remains rung 1: the company says production shipments began in August, its guide gives a substantial Q3 revenue contribution, and customers and suppliers report Vera Rubin systems in production or shipping. These facts make recognition in Q3 and disclosure by the due date more likely than not, although the evidence shown does not itself establish that revenue has already been recognized. Supply constraints and the AMI orders' later delivery schedule temper the forecast but do not overturn it; there is no evidence of abandonment to support rung 2.\"\n   }\n  }\n ]\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-figures-controls.json\\\")); print(\\\"STATEMENTS\\\"); [print(s[\\\"ref\\\"],s[\\\"day\\\"],s[\\\"statement\\\"]) for s in p[\\\"statements\\\"]]; print(\\\"QUESTIONS\\\"); [print(q[\\\"ref\\\"],list(q.keys()),q.get(\\\"day\\\"),q.get(\\\"answer\\\")) for q in p[\\\"questions\\\"]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-figures-controls.json\\\")); print(\\\"STATEMENTS\\\"); [print(s[\\\"ref\\\"],s[\\\"day\\\"],s[\\\"statement\\\"]) for s in p[\\\"statements\\\"]]; print(\\\"QUESTIONS\\\"); [print(q[\\\"ref\\\"],list(q.keys()),q.get(\\\"day\\\"),q.get(\\\"answer\\\")) for q in p[\\\"questions\\\"]]'\"","aggregated_output":"STATEMENTS\nS1 2026-10-04 NVIDIA generated $96.22 billion of second-quarter revenue and $63.73 billion of operating income, an operating margin of about 66%, according to the article.\nS2 2026-09-22 NVIDIA's latest reported quarter generated $89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion in total revenue, so roughly 92.5% of sales came from the infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI, according to GuruFocus's recounting of the results in its 22 September 2026 article.\nS3 2026-09-24 NVIDIA's gross margin is guided to settle in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, lowered because memory prices have risen more than management expected, partly helped by price increases the company has already executed.\nS4 2026-09-28 NVIDIA said on Monday 28 September 2026 that it authorized an additional $150 billion for its share repurchase program, taking the total authorization to $235 billion, which it described as the largest increase in a share repurchase authorization in history; the company said it expects to complete the total remaining buyback program through fiscal 2028.\nS5 2026-09-29 NVIDIA has a $150 billion share buyback plan, described by Yahoo Finance as the largest ever, which the article cites alongside its cash generation as justification for the repurchase programme.\nS6 2026-09-24 NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 first-quarter revenue growth of 85% year over year, with Data Center revenues up 92% year over year, as retold by Zacks Equity Research in its Analyst Blog highlights.\nS7 2026-09-25 NVIDIA reset its gross margin expectations after memory prices rose more than it expected, guiding gross margin to 74% for fiscal Q3 2027, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, and expecting gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027.\nS8 2026-09-27 NVIDIA says its $108 billion revenue forecast for the current quarter assumes no China data-center chip revenue; any China data-center chip sales would come on top of that outlook.\nS9 2026-09-23 NVIDIA's second-quarter net income rose 126% year-on-year to $59.7bn, according to the Yahoo Finance UK article, which notes earnings grew roughly 4.5 times faster than the share price over the same comparison.\nS10 2026-10-01 NVIDIA reported Q2 FY2027 EPS of $2.22, topping the $2.09 consensus and marking its fifth consecutive beat, according to the article.\nS11 2026-10-04 Nvidia reported free cash flow of $27.02 billion in the fiscal year ended January 2024, $60.85 billion in fiscal 2025 and $96.68 billion in fiscal 2026.\nS12 2026-09-29 NVIDIA's free cash flow fell by more than half in the quarter before the 29 September 2026 article, mostly because accounts receivable rose by more than $22 billion; the article states the sales were booked but the cash had not yet been collected.\nS13 2026-09-25 Fool.com reports that Nvidia's sales to two direct customers represented 22% and 14% of total revenue, respectively, in fiscal 2026, and that two customers make up more than a third of sales — a customer concentration it flags as a risk.\nS14 2026-09-23 Nvidia, in its quarterly results reported in the month before this 23 September 2026 Barchart article, posted what the article calls a market-beating fiscal Q2 and issued guidance for about 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028.\nS15 2026-10-02 NVIDIA management's gross margin outlook remains at 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points, according to Insider Monkey; the item gives no period or statement date for the outlook, and the author adds that holding that margin requires absorbing rising high-bandwidth memory costs and higher R&D spending.\nS16 2026-10-01 NVIDIA generated $24.08 billion in operating cash flow and $21.34 billion in free cash flow during fiscal Q2 2027.\nS17 2026-09-22 NVIDIA management guided fiscal third-quarter revenue to approximately $108 billion, which the article says implies 89% growth.\nS18 2026-09-23 Nvidia told shareholders in May 2026 that it would raise its quarterly dividend to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share, an increase the Morningstar author calculates at 2,400%. The author estimates roughly $25 billion per year would be returned to shareholders through the dividend, which would make Nvidia one of the largest dividend payers in the US market; that annual figure is the author's approximation, not a company-disclosed total.\nS19 2026-07-07 Broadcom AI semiconductor revenue was $10.8 billion in Q2, up 143% year over year.\nS20 2026-09-29 Nvidia reported free cash flow of $69.9 billion for the first half of fiscal 2027, 77% higher than a year before, or around $35 billion a quarter; CEO Jensen Huang linked the buyback increase to the company's \"cash generation\" in the announcement.\nS21 2026-09-28 Former Wall Street analyst David Bennett, writing on X.com and quoted by the article, stated that Nvidia generated $74.4 billion of operating cash flow in the first half of fiscal year 2027 alone while still returning $46.1 billion to shareholders, which he said lets the company fund AI expansion and buy back stock at the same time.\nS22 2026-09-22 NVIDIA's revenue more than doubled year over year in fiscal Q2 2027.\nS23 2026-09-25 NVIDIA's board approved raising the quarterly dividend from $0.01 to $0.25 per share in May 2026, a 2,400% increase that lifted the quarterly payout from about $243 million (fiscal 2027 first quarter, when the penny dividend was still in force) to approximately $6.0 billion. The first $0.25 payment went out on 26 June 2026 to holders of record as of 4 June, and a second $0.25 distribution went ex-dividend on 10 September 2026 with payment scheduled for 1 October 2026; the new annual rate is $1 per share.\nS24 2026-10-05 NVIDIA spent $20 billion on share repurchases in its most recent quarter and $40 billion in the first half of fiscal 2027, split evenly at $20 billion in each of the first and second quarters, according to the article.\nS25 2026-09-29 Nvidia's diluted share count for its fiscal 2027 second quarter, which ended July 26, 2026, was only about 1% smaller than a year before despite almost $40 billion of buybacks in the fiscal year's first half; earnings per share more than doubled.\nS26 2026-09-28 NVIDIA said its revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure, based on its reference designs, has risen from roughly $18 billion during the Hopper generation to $25 billion with Blackwell and $40 billion with Rubin.\nS27 2026-10-04 NVIDIA says Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin and that Vera Rubin delivers 35 times lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra; NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion of revenue per gigawatt for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion per gigawatt for Vera Rubin.\nS28 2026-09-29 In NVIDIA's fiscal Q2 2027, sales to the largest cloud companies were $49 billion, up 13% from the prior quarter, while sales to all other data center customers were $40 billion, up 25% from the prior quarter.\nS29 2026-09-28 Trefis reports that NVIDIA's operating margin — the share of revenue left after running costs — is 65%, the highest in ten years, versus a three-year average of 59% (the article does not state the exact measurement period).\nS30 2026-09-27 NVIDIA has already set price increases that take effect in fiscal Q1 2028, and management expects the higher prices to lift gross margin to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028.\nS31 2026-09-27 The low end of NVIDIA management's gross margin guidance for fiscal Q3 2027 is 73.5%, per the article.\nS32 2026-09-22 fool.com reports that NVIDIA generated $192.9 billion in net income over the trailing 12 months, compared with $6.4 billion for AMD, citing this in a valuation comparison of the two AI chip makers.\nS33 2026-09-25 Fool.com reports that Nvidia's fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, produced revenue of nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year, net income of approximately $120.1 billion, and a net margin of approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin of the previous fiscal year.\nS34 2026-10-02 In its latest reported quarter, Nvidia spent $19.7 billion on share buybacks and $6 billion on dividends, versus Apple's $25.9 billion of buybacks and $4 billion of dividends in Apple's fiscal 2026 third quarter; a year earlier, in the second quarter of Nvidia's fiscal 2026, Nvidia's buybacks and dividends combined totaled $10 billion.\nS35 2026-09-27 NVIDIA management expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\nS36 2026-07-12 Broadcom plans to ramp production of custom AI chips for Meta Platforms, Anthropic, and OpenAI from late 2026 into 2027.\nS37 2026-10-01 NVIDIA returned approximately $25.78 billion through share repurchases and dividends in fiscal Q2 2027, and in late September 2026 increased its share repurchase authorization by $150 billion, leaving a total of $235 billion available for future buybacks.\nS38 2026-09-26 Nvidia estimates that its revenue growth for fiscal 2027 will be around 70%, according to Fool.com contributor Keithen Drury.\nS39 2026-09-29 Nvidia management said demand for Vera Rubin currently implies its CPU revenue will more than double in fiscal 2028, which would position the company as one of the world's leading server CPU suppliers, according to the article.\nS40 2026-09-23 Nvidia announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its May 2026 dividend declaration, per the Morningstar article.\nS41 2026-09-24 NVIDIA management said Vera Rubin, its newest platform, is expected to be the fastest product ramp in the company's history, and put NVIDIA's revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin versus about $25 billion per gigawatt with Blackwell.\nS42 2026-10-05 NVIDIA's data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, up from $39 billion in the quarter of the lost H20 sales.\nS43 2026-09-28 Nvidia repurchased approximately $34 billion of its shares in fiscal year 2025, over $40.4 billion in fiscal year 2026, and $39 billion in the first half of fiscal year 2027 (the six months through July 2026), per the article.\nS44 2026-07-06 Bloomberg Intelligence forecast custom ASICs to grow at a 27% CAGR through 2033, compared with 16% for AI accelerators.\nS45 2026-10-02 On its August 2026 earnings call, Nvidia said it returned 60% of first-half fiscal 2027 free cash flow to shareholders, ahead of its 50% target, and that going forward it intends to increase the return of free cash flow net of strategic uses.\nS46 2026-09-29 NVIDIA's sales grew 83% in the twelve months to fiscal Q2 2027, per Trefis.\nS47 2026-10-01 Nvidia's revenue and profit climbed in the triple digits in its latest three-month period, to $96 billion in revenue and $59 billion in profit, according to the article, continuing the double- and triple-digit quarterly growth it says Nvidia delivered in recent years.\nS48 2026-07-06 Amazon disclosed that its Trainium business has reached multi-billion-dollar scale.\nS49 2026-10-01 In fiscal Q2 2027, NVIDIA's hyperscale revenues increased 117% year over year to $48.71 billion, while ACIE revenues, which include AI clouds, industrial and enterprise customers, jumped 138% to $40.31 billion.\nS50 2026-09-25 Nvidia's revenue rose 96% year over year to $178 billion and net income surged 161% to $118 billion in the first half of its fiscal 2027, the period ended July 26, 2026, according to the article.\nS51 2026-09-26 The article states that Nvidia's data center business is still growing 117% year over year, without specifying which quarter the growth rate refers to.\nS52 2026-09-27 Nvidia's China H200 sales remain below 1% of data-center revenue, and CEO Jensen Huang has said the company's share of China's advanced AI chip market effectively fell from around 95% to zero as restrictions tightened.\nS53 2026-09-30 Nvidia told investors in its prior-quarter report that it expects 70% revenue growth in its next fiscal year, citing massive AI demand, according to The Motley Fool.\nS54 2026-09-29 Nvidia's Grace CPUs, launched in 2021, generated over $5 billion in revenue over the last year, the article reports.\nS55 2026-09-29 Nvidia expects $20 billion in CPU sales this year, according to the article.\nS56 2026-09-22 NVIDIA's gross margin came in at 75% in the last quarter, as reported by qz.com.\nS57 2026-09-30 NVIDIA's gross margin was 75% in fiscal second-quarter 2027 and is expected to be in the 72% to 73% range for fiscal 2028, with memory costs having risen more than management expected, as reported by Fool.com.\nS58 2026-07-06 Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, up more than 200% year over year.\nS59 2026-09-24 NVIDIA raised its dividend 25-fold in June and set a policy to return at least 50% of free cash flow to shareholders, according to its public June disclosures.\nS60 2026-09-22 NVIDIA's days of sales outstanding, the average time to collect cash after a sale, increased to 60 days; management said this reflects extended payment terms for large purchases by certain investment-grade customers, shipped over multiple quarters.\nS61 2026-09-30 NVIDIA's operating margin stands at 65%, a rate the article says the company has roughly maintained for the last two years.\nS62 2026-09-25 Zacks states that NVIDIA's net profit margin is 63.7%, compared with Micron's 55.9%, and that NVIDIA's return on equity of 96% exceeds Micron's 72.5%.\nS63 2026-09-30 On Nvidia's Q2 earnings call, executives said the company had begun shipping a small volume of H200 accelerators to China but that these shipments amounted to less than 1 percent of its data-center revenues.\nS64 2026-10-02 Nvidia's non-GAAP EPS was $2.22 in fiscal Q2 2027, up from $1.59 in fiscal Q4 2026, a gain of about 40% in two quarters; its adjusted EPS for the latest four reported quarters was around $6.95 versus $4.64 for fiscal 2026, up about 50%. Nvidia now treats stock-based compensation as a cost in adjusted earnings and restated earlier quarters to match, according to the article.\nS65 2026-09-22 NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, a figure the article says far exceeded the 45% Wall Street had previously modeled.\nS66 2026-10-05 NVIDIA itself expects its volume sales to double, an expectation the MarketBeat article ties to the Vera Rubin release and to the next wave of data center construction.\nS67 2026-10-05 NVIDIA returned $26.0 billion to shareholders in the quarter and had $99.0 billion remaining under its buyback authorization.\nQUESTIONS\nQ13 ['request', 'answer', 'ref'] None {'rung': 1, 'implied_outcome': 'The implied calendar-2026 spending remains below $40 billion. Marvell’s $75 billion is potential lifetime revenue across more than 50 opportunities with no specified timeframe, not 2026 spending; Meta’s reported September manufacturing start gives no production volume or spend figure, implying at most an unquantified late-year contribution. v6’s neutral NVIDIA-share conclusion is consistent with custom silicon taking some share, but does not quantify its spending.', 'reasons': 'No quantified market consensus, company guidance, or stated 2026 spending plan is supplied, so there is no reference rung to place on the ladder. The new evidence points to some 2026 custom-silicon activity, but neither item quantifies calendar-2026 provider spending; the implied outcome remains below $40 billion rather than crossing the $40 billion boundary.'}\nQ4 ['ref', 'request', 'answer'] None {'rung': 1, 'implied_outcome': 'The most likely outcome is that NVIDIA recognizes Vera Rubin revenue in Q3 FY2027 and discloses it in its quarterly results, with the guided contribution of about 20% of Data Center revenue. This is inferred from the guide together with production shipments beginning in August and customer production deployments; it is not yet established by a reported result in the evidence shown.', 'reasons': \"The reference is NVIDIA management's expectation that Vera Rubin will account for about 20% of Data Center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027; because Q3 results are due before 2027-01-31, that reference sits at rung 1. The forecast remains rung 1: the company says production shipments began in August, its guide gives a substantial Q3 revenue contribution, and customers and suppliers report Vera Rubin systems in production or shipping. These facts make recognition in Q3 and disclosure by the due date more likely than not, although the evidence shown does not itself establish that revenue has already been recognized. Supply constraints and the AMI orders' later delivery schedule temper the forecast but do not overturn it; there is no evidence of abandonment to support rung 2.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-figures-controls.json\\\")); [print(\\\"QUESTION\\\",q[\\\"ref\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",q[\\\"request\\\"]) for q in p[\\\"questions\\\"]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-figures-controls.json\\\")); [print(\\\"QUESTION\\\",q[\\\"ref\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",q[\\\"request\\\"]) for q in p[\\\"questions\\\"]]'\"","aggregated_output":"QUESTION Q13 \n Thesis v16 (custom-silicon, theme, focus \"Spending on custom AI silicon (ASICs) at cloud providers in calendar 2026\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): How much will cloud providers spend on custom AI silicon (ASICs) in calendar 2026?\nLadder: rung 1 Below $40 billion: Custom-silicon spending is less than $40 billion. / rung 2 $40 billion to below $60 billion: Custom-silicon spending is at least $40 billion and less than $60 billion. / rung 3 $60 billion to below $80 billion: Custom-silicon spending is at least $60 billion and less than $80 billion. / rung 4 $80 billion to below $100 billion: Custom-silicon spending is at least $80 billion and less than $100 billion. / rung 5 $100 billion or more: Custom-silicon spending is at least $100 billion.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 2 items]\n- e327 [rumour; moomoo.com; original source Meta ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Meta reportedly plans to start manufacturing its AI chip in September 2026.\n    Quote: Meta Platforms (META.US)$ is reportedly planning to start manufacturing its AI chip in September, Reuters reported Thursday, citing the company’s internal memo.\n- e502 [party_statement; Fool.com analyst, reporting Marvell’s statement; original source Marvell Technology ; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] Marvell says it has more than 50 potential custom AI chip opportunities across over 10 customers, representing potential lifetime revenue of $75 billion; no timeframe beyond “in the long run” is specified.\n    Quote: In all, Marvell notes that it can tap into more than 50 custom AI chip opportunities across over 10 customers in the long run, leading to potential lifetime revenue of $75 billion.\n\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v6 (open; last_end) What share of calendar-2026 data-center AI accelerator spending will NVIDIA hold, by the first published full-year estimate?: now rung 3 Neutral; reasons: The closest market reference is the estimate that NVIDIA’s AI-chip share will be 75% in 2026, which is rung 3; longer-term estimates of 70%–75% and near 75% point to the same rung, while undated 80% estimates are less specific. The implied outcome is about 75%: those estimates concern the broader AI-chip market rather than precisely calendar-2026 data-center accelerator spending, but they are the best quantified proxies supplied. NVIDIA’s strong training position and Huawei’s low performance-adjusted share support a higher outcome; the lower inference share, growing custom-silicon alternatives\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: No supplied consensus, company guidance, or stated spending plan gives a market reference or a quantified 2026 spending estimate. The reported September manufacturing start is late in the year and gives no production volume or spending figure; it therefore does not imply that custom-ASIC spending reaches $40 billion. v6's neutral NVIDIA-share conclusion is consistent with custom ASICs taking some share, but does not quantify custom-ASIC spending. The most likely outcome remains below $40 billion.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above.\nQUESTION Q4 \n Thesis v4 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Whether Vera Rubin moves from full production to volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within NVIDIA's fiscal 2027, as shown in the company's quarterly results and Form 10-K.\", 2027, due 2027-01-31): Will NVIDIA recognize revenue from Vera Rubin volume customer shipments by the end of FY2027, as disclosed in its quarterly results or FY2027 Form 10-K?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: NVIDIA's quarterly results or FY2027 Form 10-K disclose that it has begun recognizing revenue from Vera Rubin volume customer shipments by 2027-01-31. / rung 2 Ruled out: After 2026-09-23 and before 2027-01-31, NVIDIA formally abandons or cancels the Vera Rubin platform. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-23 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 53 items]\n[Not shown: 37 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n- e85 [party_statement; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:44b5d56e3c6718335d33ab2b 2026-09-23] NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform has entered the full production stage, positioning the company to benefit from the next phase of AI infrastructure investment, as reported by Zacks.\n    Quote: Additionally, the next-generation Vera Rubin platform has entered the full production stage, positioning NVIDIA to benefit from the next phase of AI infrastructure investment.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e90, e185, e251, e283, e291, e320, e343; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e89 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source Nebius Group; CoreWeave ; from gnews:a5837f6a8cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] Nebius Group announced another increase in prices for NVIDIA GPU rentals, and CoreWeave said it was continuing to contract new compute capacity at higher prices; the article cites both as fresh evidence that demand for NVIDIA-powered computing remains strong. No price levels, capacity amounts or dates are given.\n    Quote: Meanwhile, demand from AI cloud providers remains robust as Nebius Group (NBIS) announced another increase in prices for Nvidia GPU rentals, while CoreWeave (CRWV) said it was continuing to contract new compute capacity at higher prices, providing fresh evidence that demand for Nvidia-powered computing remains strong.\n- e90 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA management 2026-08-26; from gnews:a5837f6a8cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] NVIDIA management pointed to full production of its next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, and the article says continued AI infrastructure strength is accelerating NVIDIA's AI growth trajectory. The statement does not say Vera Rubin has begun volume customer shipments or revenue recognition.\n    Quote: It's worth noting that Nvidia is not merely maintaining its AI growth trajectory; it is accelerating it, with management pointing to full production of its next-generation Vera Rubin platform and continued strength across AI infrastructure.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e181 [fact; GuruFocus (gurufocus.com); original source Super Micro Computer 2026-09-24; from gnews:cd0056006ad115260ae2b646 2026-09-24] Super Micro Computer moved into commercial shipments of servers built around NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform, shipping Vera Rubin NVL72 racks through its Data Center Building Block Solutions that combine computing hardware with liquid cooling, networking, storage and power components; each rack design uses 72 Rubin GPUs and 36 Vera CPUs, according to a GuruFocus report published 24 September 2026.\n    Quote: the server maker moved into commercial shipments of systems built around NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform, adding another data-center product to its AI infrastructure lineup. … The company is shipping Vera Rubin NVL72 racks through its Data Center Building Block Solutions, combining computing hardware with liquid cooling, networking, storage and power components. The rack design uses 7\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e859, e951; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e188 [party_statement; fool.com (article via Yahoo Finance); original source SpaceX (Elon Musk, CEO) ; from gnews:943a57daed49d866fe468605 2026-09-24] On SpaceX's second-quarter earnings call, SpaceX CEO Elon Musk said SpaceX has decided to build exclusively on NVIDIA hardware because he considers the Vera Rubin architecture the best architecture and the best AI computer, citing close cooperation and partnership with NVIDIA; the article characterises this as making SpaceX (which owns xAI and its Grok model) exclusive to NVIDIA.\n    Quote: During SpaceX's second-quarter earnings call, Musk had this to say about SpaceX's relationship with Nvidia: … We've decided to build exclusively on NVIDIA because we think the Vera Rubin architecture is the best architecture. We think it's the best AI computer, and we greatly value our close cooperation and partnership on many levels with NVIDIA. So, we're exclusive to NVIDIA.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e387, e396; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e208 [fact; Zacks (zacks.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7fb0b2b7d22b8bb07625b212 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform began production shipments in the third quarter, according to Zacks; the item gives no volume, customer or revenue-recognition figure for the shipments.\n    Quote: Vera Rubin began production shipments in the third quarter.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e234, e409, e598, e650, e721, e857; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e234 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA began production shipments of its Vera Rubin platform in August, with purchase orders already received from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system OEM, according to a 24 September 2026 article.\n    Quote: Production shipments began in August, with purchase orders already in from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud, and system OEM.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e208; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e236 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA (CFO) ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's CFO expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme; the article reports memory prices have risen more than management expected.\n    Quote: The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme. … Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e271, e413, e414, e556, e653, e774, e864, e865; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e243 [fact; Kieron Allen, Cloud Wars (cloudwars.com); original source Amazon Web Services and NVIDIA (joint announcement) ; from gnews:7c4283c55cf02ba9cfd9a835 2026-09-24] Amazon Web Services and NVIDIA announced an expansion of their long-standing partnership under which AWS will deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs across its infrastructure between 2027 and 2028, spanning NVIDIA's Blackwell Ultra, Rubin and Rubin Ultra architectures and deployed alongside NVIDIA Vera CPUs; the addition builds on the more than 1 million NVIDIA GPUs AWS had previously committed to deploying beginning in 2026, and Amazon said the aim is to meet surging demand for AI infrastructure from frontier labs, global enterprises, start-ups and governments. The number of GPUs is a planned deployment, not reported deliveries or revenue.\n    Quote: Now, the two powerhouses have announced a staggering expansion of this existing partnership which will, among other things — but this is certainly the standout headline — include the deployment of 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs across AWS infrastructure between 2027 and 2028. … The aim, according to Amazon, is to meet surging demand for AI infrastructure “from frontier labs, global enterprises, \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e105; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e279 [third_party_view; qz.com, citing MarketWatch; original source Mehdi Hosseini, Susquehanna analyst (remarks to MarketWatch) ; from gnews:460160402f7a10882d1e925f 2026-09-25] Susquehanna analyst Mehdi Hosseini, in remarks reported by MarketWatch and cited by qz.com, said Micron's November-quarter (fiscal Q1 2026) sales forecast is strong enough to satisfy investors but will not fully reflect the benefits of the Nvidia product ramp; he said some estimated revenue is expected to shift toward next year instead of this year, implying the Nvidia-chip-related revenue payoff may be delayed.\n    Quote: Micron $MU Technology posted record fiscal fourth-quarter revenue and issued strong guidance for the current quarter, but one analyst says investors may need to wait longer to see the full payoff from an Nvidia $NVDA chip launch. … Susquehanna analyst Mehdi Hosseini told MarketWatch that Micron 's November-quarter sales forecast looks strong enough to satisfy investors, though it won't fully refle\n- e283 [party_statement; MarketBeat; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:91b917994e36036e304616e8 2026-09-25] NVIDIA states that its Vera Rubin AI platform is already in full production, with production shipments beginning in August 2026 and purchase orders received from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system original equipment manufacturer (OEM), as reported by MarketBeat.\n    Quote: The trigger for this move could come from subsequent earnings reports and continued progress with Vera Rubin. The platform is already in full production, with NVIDIA reporting production shipments beginning in August and purchase orders from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system original equipment manufacturer (OEM).\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e370 [fact; cryptorank.io; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:844106b7a25ecc1175290290 2026-09-26] Nvidia reported that in the latest MLPerf Inference v6.1 tests its next-generation Vera Rubin NVL72 delivered up to 3.7x the throughput of its GB300 NVL72 on the Qwen3-VL benchmark and up to 2.5x on DeepSeek-R1, and described the Vera Rubin entry in its official MLPerf announcement as a preview submission ahead of broader deployment.\n    Quote: In the latest MLPerf Inference v6.1 tests, Vera Rubin NVL72 delivered up to 3.7x the throughput of Nvidia’s GB300 NVL72 on the Qwen3-VL benchmark. On DeepSeek-R1, the improvement reached as much as 2.5x. … Nvidia detailed the results in its official MLPerf announcement , describing Vera Rubin’s entry as a preview submission ahead of broader deployment.\n- e409 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA began production shipments of its newest data center system, Vera Rubin, in August 2026, according to the article.\n    Quote: The company began production shipments of Vera Rubin, its newest data center system, in August 2026.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e208; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e410 [party_statement; NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA management expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\n    Quote: Management expects Vera Rubin to bring in about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e555, e764, e782, e813, e858; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e416 [party_statement; NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA management expects sales to its largest cloud companies to speed up again in fiscal Q4 2027 as Vera Rubin supply grows.\n    Quote: Management expects sales to the largest cloud companies to speed up again in fiscal Q4 2027, as Vera Rubin supply grows.\n- e551 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4334522792e44c6fd9d18ccd 2026-09-28] NVIDIA management said on its fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call that inventory rose to $32 billion as the company prepared for the Vera Rubin launch.\n    Quote: On the fiscal Q2 2027 call, management said inventory rose to $32 billion as NVIDIA prepared for the Vera Rubin launch.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e588; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e555 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4334522792e44c6fd9d18ccd 2026-09-28] NVIDIA management expects the newly launched Vera Rubin platform to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027, according to Trefis.\n    Quote: The newly launched Vera Rubin platform is one source of that revenue. Management expects it to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e633 [third_party_view; Adam Spatacco, fool.com; original source  ; from gnews:0e4e3f9b17e0cda6b72d47fc 2026-09-29] The article reports a forecast that Nvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin CPU architecture will reach $20 billion in sales next quarter.\n    Quote: Meanwhile, the next-generation Vera Rubin CPU architecture is forecast to reach $20 billion in sales next quarter.\n- e659 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation (NVIDIA Blog); original source CoreWeave ; from nvblog:coreweave-agentic-ai-vera-rubin 2026-09-30] CoreWeave announced at its CoreWeave Fully Connected event (held the week of the 30 September 2026 NVIDIA blog post) that NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems with Spectrum-X 102.4T Ethernet networking are available on CoreWeave Cloud, making CoreWeave one of the first cloud providers to deliver the platform in customers' hands; per the announcement, Cognition, the applied AI lab behind the Devin AI software engineer, is the first customer running production workloads on Vera Rubin.\n    Quote: At CoreWeave Fully Connected, running this week in San Francisco, CoreWeave announced availability of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems with Spectrum-X 102.4T Ethernet networking. Cognition, the applied AI lab behind the Devin AI software engineer, is the first customer running production workloads on Vera Rubin. … CoreWeave announced availability of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 on CoreWeave Cloud, makin\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e751, e860, e894, e895; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e660 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation (NVIDIA Blog); original source CoreWeave ; from nvblog:coreweave-agentic-ai-vera-rubin 2026-09-30] CoreWeave received its first NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 production racks earlier in September 2026, before the 30 September 2026 article.\n    Quote: Earlier this month, CoreWeave received its first Vera Rubin NVL72 production racks.\n- e720 [fact; CRN; original source TD Synnex (CEO Patrick Zammit), with customer identity reported by CRN ; from gnews:84569ea837e5d2d93ff16093 2026-09-30] TD Synnex CEO Patrick Zammit said on the distributor's third-quarter earnings call (the Thursday before the 30 September 2026 article) that TD Synnex and Houston-based systems integrator Mark III Systems recently signed an agreement to support an NVIDIA AI factory powered by Vera Rubin NVL72 systems, which he described as 'one of the largest' enterprise AI factory infrastructure deployments expected to be delivered through the channel, bundling design, integration, deployment, Day 2 co-admin operations, financing and supply chain. CRN reported, citing what it learned, that the customer is a US-based Fortune 100 company. No contract value, system count or delivery date was disclosed.\n    Quote: After TD Synnex revealed that Mark III Systems is set to deliver ‘one of the largest’ enterprise AI factory deployments in the channel, an executive at the systems integrator explained to CRN how its AI factory expertise and enterprise trends helped it seal the deal. … As hyperscalers and neoclouds begin to launch offerings based on Nvidia’s new flagship Vera Rubin rack-scale AI platform, one syst\n- e751 [fact; GuruFocus (gurufocus.com); original source CoreWeave ; from gnews:2b6641fb175eb6c57e2bdff2 2026-09-30] CoreWeave (CRWV) announced at its Fully Connected AI conference in San Francisco that it is bringing NVIDIA (NVDA) Vera CPUs into its cloud portfolio for AI-agent workloads and will offer NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems for production use, with Cognition named as the first customer running workloads on the platform. CoreWeave said the Rubin systems are already operating on its cloud service, which it described as a move from infrastructure deployment toward commercial use, and that customers can access the hardware under its existing operating model for NVIDIA systems.\n    Quote: The company is bringing NVIDIA ( NVDA ) Vera CPUs to its cloud portfolio for workloads tied to AI agents. It will also offer NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems for production use, with Cognition serving as the first customer running workloads on the platform. … CoreWeave said the Rubin systems are already operating on its cloud service, marking a move from infrastructure deployment toward commercial \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e659; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e753 [third_party_view; NVIDIA Corporation (NVIDIA Blog); original source SemiAnalysis (AgentX data) ; from nvblog:productive-durable-fungible-ai-factories 2026-10-01] SemiAnalysis AgentX data cited in NVIDIA's blog states that NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems deliver over 30x higher throughput per megawatt than NVIDIA GB300 NVL72 systems, and up to 45x lower cost per million tokens on the DeepSeek V4 Pro model.\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis AgentX data shows NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems deliver over 30x higher throughput per megawatt than NVIDIA GB300 NVL72, and up to 45x lower cost per million tokens on the DeepSeek V4 Pro model.\n- e764 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA said it started shipping its Vera Rubin platform in August 2026 and expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of Data Center revenues in fiscal Q3 2027.\n    Quote: NVIDIA started shipping Vera Rubin in August and expects the platform to account for about 20% of Data Center revenues in the third quarter.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e765 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA estimates revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure at approximately $18 billion for Hopper, $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\n    Quote: The company estimates that revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure has increased from approximately $18 billion with Hopper to $25 billion with Blackwell and $40 billion with Vera Rubin.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e488; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e775 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform is now in full production, and the article states revenue per gigawatt of deployed capacity has risen from $18 billion for Hopper to $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\n    Quote: Revenue per gigawatt rose from $18 billion for Hopper to $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin, now in full production.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e488; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e782 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source  ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] The article estimates Vera Rubin will make up about 20% of NVIDIA's data center revenue in Q3 FY2027, with NVIDIA's next earnings report due November 17.\n    Quote: Vera Rubin should make up about 20% of data center revenue in Q3, and the next earnings report lands November 17.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e811 [fact; fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:efb160acb7c94e14a52df9fa 2026-10-01] Nvidia launched its Vera CPU, a central processing unit positioned for agentic-AI workloads; the article reports the launch while noting that the agentic AI boom will likely raise CPU demand. The item gives no launch date, volume, customer or shipment timing.\n    Quote: There is a significant opportunity in some of the niches it targets. For instance, the agentic AI boom will likely lead to increased demand for CPUs (central processing units), and to that end, Nvidia launched its Vera CPU.\n- e813 [party_statement; Daniel Foelber, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:e84d920e300dc3c29e501037 2026-10-01] Nvidia began shipping its Rubin platform in August and expects Rubin to account for 20% of data center revenue in the third quarter of fiscal 2027, which the company will report in November, according to the article.\n    Quote: Nvidia began shipping Rubin in August and already expects it to account for 20% of data center revenue in the third quarter of fiscal 2027, which it will report in November.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e814 [party_statement; Daniel Foelber, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:e84d920e300dc3c29e501037 2026-10-01] Nvidia is guiding for 70% year-over-year revenue growth in fiscal 2028, which the article attributes to massive demand for its Rubin platform.\n    Quote: Demand for Rubin is so massive that Nvidia is guiding for 70% year-over-year revenue growth in fiscal 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e104; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e832 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, fool.com; original source Keithen Drury (fool.com) 2026-10-01; from gnews:6793603da478ba6858e0827d 2026-10-01] In his 1 October 2026 column, Keithen Drury (fool.com) wrote that Nvidia's new Rubin-generation chips are starting to become available, will provide more advanced capabilities than prior generations, and that Nvidia will charge a higher price for them, which he called a built-in revenue escalator 'launching shortly'. No shipment volumes, dates or prices are given, and the claim is the author's characterisation rather than a company announcement.\n    Quote: Even though competition is intensifying for which company has the best available option, Nvidia's new Rubin generation of chips will make an even greater impact than prior iterations. These computing units are starting to become available and will provide more advanced capabilities than prior generations. Nvidia will also charge a higher price tag for them, so it has a built-in revenue escalator t\n- e857 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management said on the fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call that the company began production shipments of its Vera Rubin AI data-center platform in August 2026.\n    Quote: NVIDIA began production shipments in August 2026, management said on the fiscal Q2 2027 call.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e208; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e858 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management forecast on the fiscal Q2 2027 call that Vera Rubin would make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027; the article notes this is a forecast, not a result.\n    Quote: Management expects Vera Rubin to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027. That is a forecast, not a result.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e859 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Supermicro 2026-09-23; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] Supermicro said on 23 September 2026 that it is shipping racks built on NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform.\n    Quote: Supermicro said on September 23, 2026 that it is shipping racks built on Vera Rubin.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e181; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e860 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source CoreWeave 2026-09-30; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] CoreWeave, a cloud company, said on 30 September 2026 that one of its customers runs production workloads on NVIDIA's Vera Rubin system.\n    Quote: CoreWeave, a cloud company, said on September 30 that one of its customers runs production workloads on the system.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e659; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e864 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management says the company cannot build enough to meet demand, called its fiscal 2028 forecast